@Tutorial(time: 50) {
    @Intro(title: "Performance Optimisation") {
        Optimise ATL transformations for better performance with large models and complex metamodels.

        In this tutorial, you'll learn performance best practices, identify common bottlenecks, and apply optimisation techniques.

        You'll discover how to write efficient helpers, optimise collection operations, and structure transformations for maximum performance.

        @Image(source: "atl-transformations.svg", alt: "ATL Transformations")
    }

    @Section(title: "Identifying Performance Issues") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Performance problems typically arise from inefficient collection operations, redundant computations, and poorly structured helpers.

            Following best practices prevents most performance issues before they occur.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the source metamodel that the transformations of this tutorial read.

                @Code(name: "Source.ecore", file: atl-08-step-01-source-metamodel.ecore)

                The transformations of this tutorial read Containers, each with a `name` and a list of `elements`. An Element has a `name`, an `active` flag and an integer `priority`, and a SpecialElement is an Element with a `specialValue` of its own. The helpers filter and count the elements of a container by these features. Save it as Source.ecore, where the `-- @path` directives of the transformations find it.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the target metamodel for the items that the transformations produce.

                @Code(name: "Target.ecore", file: atl-08-step-02-target-metamodel.ecore)

                The rules of this tutorial create Items and SpecialItems of the Target metamodel. An Item binds `name`, the integer `count` and `names`, which receives one string for each active element, so `names` is a many-valued `EString` attribute rather than a reference. A SpecialItem binds `name` and `specialProperty`, both strings. Save it as Target.ecore beside Source.ecore.
            }

            @Step {
                Identify inefficient patterns.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-03-inefficient-patterns.atl)

                Common anti-patterns include nested iterations, redundant filtering, and repeated collection traversals.
            }

            @Step {
                Cache expensive computations.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-04-caching-results.atl)

                Store expensive computation results in helpers to avoid recalculation.
            }

            @Step {
                Optimise collection operations.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-05-optimised-collections.atl)

                Combine the conditions of successive filters into one `select`. Every `select` traverses its collection and builds a new one, so two `select` operations in a row traverse the elements twice and build an intermediate collection that only the second one reads.
            }

            @Step {
                Use appropriate rule types.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-06-efficient-rules.atl)

                Choose matched, lazy, or called rules based on transformation requirements.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Advanced Optimisation Techniques") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Advanced optimisation focuses on reducing computational overhead, eliminating redundant operations, and measuring actual performance improvements.

            These techniques target specific bottlenecks in transformation execution.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Minimise helper call overhead.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-07-helper-optimisation.atl)

                Reduce the number of helper calls in performance-critical sections.
            }

            @Step {
                Avoid unnecessary type checking.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-08-type-optimisation.atl)

                Use guard conditions instead of repeated type checks in bindings.
            }

            @Step {
                Profile and measure improvements.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-09-profiling.atl)

                `thisModule.getCurrentTime()` is an extension of swift-atl, not part of Eclipse ATL, that returns the current time in milliseconds since the Unix epoch, so the difference of two readings is the time between them in milliseconds. The `using` block reads the clock before the target element is built, and `debug` writes each reading to standard error with its label, so that the two readings show how long the rule took.
            }

            @Step {
                Build an optimised transformation.

                @Code(name: "OptimisationExample.atl", file: atl-08-step-10-complete-optimised.atl)

                The complete transformation demonstrates all optimisation techniques working together.
            }

            @Step {
                Create a sample backlog with four elements, and an empty archive, to transform.

                @Code(name: "source.xmi", file: atl-08-step-11-sample-source.xmi)

                Dark mode sets no `active` flag, so it reads as false, the default of a Boolean attribute. Release notes is a SpecialElement, so its `xsi:type` attribute names that class. The Archive container has no elements. A model with more than one root element wraps them in an `xmi:XMI` element.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the new files and run the complete transformation on the sample backlog.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-08-step-12-run-complete.sh)

                `--source IN=source.xmi` binds the model IN of the `create` line to the sample model, and `--target OUT=target.xmi` names the file that receives the model OUT. swift-atl finds `Source.ecore` and `Target.ecore` beside the transformation through its `-- @path` directives.
            }

            @Step {
                Look at the target model that the transformation wrote.

                @Code(name: "target.xmi", file: atl-08-step-13-target.xmi)

                The guard of OptimisedTransform skips the empty Archive, so the transformation creates a single Item, for the Backlog. Its `count` is the number of active elements, 3, and `names` holds their names in order, leaving out the inactive Dark mode. A model with a single root element needs no `xmi:XMI` element.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What is the most common performance bottleneck in ATL?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Nested collection iterations

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    Nested iterations have O(n²) or worse complexity. Combining operations or restructuring logic can often eliminate nesting and dramatically improve performance.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Using too many helpers

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    Helpers themselves don't cause performance problems. The issue is what operations they perform, not how many exist.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Creating too many target elements

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    Element creation is generally fast. Performance issues come from inefficient queries and redundant computations.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            When should you cache helper results?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Always, for every helper

                @Justification(reaction: "Too broad!") {
                    Only cache expensive computations that are called multiple times. Simple helpers don't need caching.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                When the helper performs expensive computations called multiple times

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                    Cache results when a helper performs expensive operations (like large collection traversals) and is called repeatedly. For simple helpers, caching adds unnecessary complexity.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Never, it doesn't improve performance

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    Caching expensive computations can significantly improve performance by eliminating redundant work.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How can you reduce collection operation overhead?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Avoid using collections entirely

                @Justification(reaction: "Impractical!") {
                    Collections are fundamental to model transformations. The goal is to use them efficiently, not avoid them.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Combine conditions so that each collection is traversed as few times as possible

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly!") {
                    Every collection operation traverses its input and builds a new collection. One `select` with the condition `a and b` does the work of `select(a)` followed by `select(b)` in a single traversal, without the intermediate collection.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Use only `forAll` and `exists`

                @Justification(reaction: "Too restrictive!") {
                    Different operations serve different purposes. The key is combining them efficiently, not limiting your toolkit.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
